{"slug":"non-plan","label":"NON PLAN","colour":"#FFA6A6","items":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","jobtitle":"Journalist and writer","recorded":"2014-11-05","hls":"https://customer-syg1o9n270h63juf.cloudflarestream.com/54132568ac7d06d0e526816f4f22ccd8/manifest/video.m3u8","start":586,"end":1030,"topicId":"non-plan","segments":[{"start":586,"end":1030,"topicId":"non-plan"}],"cards":[{"time":588.44,"title":"Non Plan","id":"non-plan"},{"time":623,"title":"Peter Hall","id":"peter-hall"},{"time":693.36,"title":"Le Corbusier","id":"le-corbusier"},{"time":824.96,"title":"Peter Reyner Banham","id":"peter-reyner-banham"}]}],"cards":{"Le Corbusier":{"slug":"le-corbusier","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; mso-bidi-font-weight: bold;\">Charles-Édouard Jeanneret-Gris</span><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\">, who was better known as Le Corbusier (French: </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA_for_French\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">[lə kɔʁbyzje]</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\">; October 6, 1887 – August 27, 1965), was a </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swiss\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">Swiss</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\">-</span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_people\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">French</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\"> architect, designer, painter, </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_planner\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">urban planner</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\">, writer, and one of the pioneers of what is now called </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_architecture\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">modern architecture</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\">. He was born in </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Switzerland\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">Switzerland</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\"> and became a </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">French citizen</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\"> in 1930. His career spanned five decades, with his buildings constructed throughout Europe, India, and America. Dedicated to providing better living conditions for the residents of crowded cities, Le Corbusier was influential in </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_planning\"><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">urban planning</span></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\">, and was a founding member of the </span><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congr%C3%A8s_International_d%27Architecture_Moderne\"><i><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: windowtext; text-decoration: none; text-underline: none;\">Congrès International d'Architecture Moderne</span></i></a><span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica;\"> (CIAM).</span></p>","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/le-corbusier.jpg","external":"http://www.fondationlecorbusier.fr","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Roquebrune-Cap-Martin,+France/@43.757808,7.473754,7z","also":[{"slug":"bernard-tschumi","name":"Bernard Tschumi","time":1142.36,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":693.36,"topicId":"non-plan","topicLabel":"NON PLAN"}]},"Non Plan":{"slug":"non-plan","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">In 1969, Cedric Price with Paul Barker (writer), Reyner Banham (architecture historian) and Peter Hall (geographer and planner) published ‘Non-Plan: an experiment in freedom’ an article in a social affairs magazine titled New Society. The idea emerged after a conversation about the appalling results of current urban planning strategies and it get any worse if there was no planning at all. At the time, Non-Plan infuriated many architects and planners because not only was it extremely provocative and contentious but it also went against the established order and controlled uniformity of the built environment.</p>","subtitle":"Paul Barker, Cedric Price, Reyner Banham, Peter Hall","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/non-plan.jpg","external":"http://www.amazon.com/Non-Plan-Freedom-Participation-Architecture-Urbanism/dp/0750640839","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":588.44,"topicId":"non-plan","topicLabel":"NON PLAN"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1193,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"}]},"Peter Hall":{"slug":"peter-hall","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Sir Peter Geoffrey Hall, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Academy\">FBA</a> (19 March 1932 – 30 July 2014) was an English <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_planning\">town planner</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urbanist\">urbanist</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_geography\">geographer</a>. He was the Bartlett Professor of Planning and Regeneration at <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bartlett\">The Bartlett</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_College_London\">University College London</a> and President of both the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Town_and_Country_Planning_Association\">Town and Country Planning Association</a> and the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regional_Studies_Association\">Regional Studies Association</a>. He was internationally renowned for his studies and writings on the economic, demographic, cultural and management issues that face cities around the globe. Hall was for many years a planning and regeneration adviser to successive UK governments. He was Special Adviser on Strategic Planning to the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_government\">British government</a> (1991–94) and a member of the Office of the Deputy Prime Minister's Urban Task Force (1998–1999). Hall is considered by many to be the father of the industrial <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enterprise_zone\">enterprise zone</a> concept, adopted by countries worldwide to develop industry in disadvantaged areas.</p>","subtitle":"Geographer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/peter-hall.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Hall_(urbanist)","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5073509,-0.1277583,7z","also":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":623,"topicId":"non-plan","topicLabel":"NON PLAN"}]},"Peter Reyner Banham":{"slug":"peter-reyner-banham","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Peter Reyner Banham, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FRIBA\">FRIBA</a> (2 March 1922 – 19 March 1988) was an English architectural critic and prolific writer best known for his theoretical treatise Theory and Design in the First Machine Age (1960) and for his 1971 book Los Angeles: The Architecture of Four Ecologies.<sup>[1]</sup> In the latter he categorized the Los Angeles experience into four ecological models (Surfurbia, Foothills, The Plains of Id, and Autopia) and explored the distinct architectural cultures of each ecology. Banham worked in London, but lived primarily in the United States from the late 1960s until the end of his life.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1340.8,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":406.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":57.84,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":824.96,"topicId":"non-plan","topicLabel":"NON PLAN"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1075.8,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1178.8,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"}]}}}